  "text": "The article argues that the first portable panels on altarpieces were intended mainly for worship purposes, not for storing relics. By looking closely at the shape and picture content of five well‑known central‑European altarpieces made in the early 1300s, the study shows that the side panels were repeatedly opened and closed to display different scenes that matched the church calendar. This pattern demonstrates that the movable wings served a liturgical role, allowing the visual program to shift with the seasons and feast days. The paper also points to surviving examples of large painted winged altarpieces created shortly after the turn of the 14th century, suggesting they form a bridge between early medieval altar designs and the more elaborate carved altarpieces that appear later in the Gothic period."
